7. CLARIFY LLAC WHEN ACTIONS ARE MISSING OR CANCELLED
======================================================================

We accept Latest Linked Action Completion as the neutral default anchor.

Use these states:

- LLAC available:
  At least one non-cancelled linked action has a valid completion date.

- No completed linked action:
  Linked actions exist, but none has a valid completion date.

- All linked actions cancelled:
  No linked action contributes to LLAC.

- No linked action recorded:
  Separate no-action cohort.

- Action-link uncertainty:
  Cross Ref and association fields disagree materially.

Issue closure date may be used as an analytical fallback anchor only if clearly labelled as such.

Do not present a fallback issue-closure anchor as the completion of remediation.

======================================================================
8. ADD TEXT-QUALITY CHECKS BEYOND LENGTH
======================================================================

The random rater review is essential and should determine narrative usability.

Add checks for:

- Placeholder text such as “N/A”, “refer attached”, “as discussed” or equivalent
- Text consisting largely of IDs, URLs or system-generated content
- Duplicated title and description
- Closure summaries copied from action descriptions
- Issue descriptions copied across many records
- Truncated text
- HTML or formatting artefacts
- Encoding problems
- Excessively long lifecycle commentary
- Contradictory statements within the same object
- Embedded tables or list structures
- References to attachments that are unavailable

For each text field, classify usability for its intended task:

- Suitable for weakness extraction
- Suitable for action-claim extraction
- Suitable for implementation-evidence review
- Suitable for verification-evidence review
- Suitable for incident retrieval
- Not suitable

A narrative may be useful for one task and unusable for another.

Therefore, one overall “usable narrative” percentage is not sufficient by itself.

======================================================================
9. CHANGE THE BOILERPLATE CHECK
======================================================================

“Appears verbatim or near-verbatim in more than 20 records” is a useful screening rule but should not be treated as inherently defective.

Standard wording may be expected for:

- Repeated control-gap templates
- Standard action requirements
- Regulatory programmes
- Bulk incidents
- Common closure attestations
- System-generated records

The profile should distinguish:

- Identical full-text records
- Shared template with meaningful variable content
- Generic wording with little case-specific content
- Repeated text caused by duplicate records
- Legitimate standard statements

Measure how much unique case-specific information remains after removing common template language.

This residual content is more informative than the frequency of repeated text alone.

======================================================================
10. ADD DATA-LEAKAGE AND LABEL-LEAKAGE CHECKS
======================================================================

Some fields may directly reveal the target judgement.

Examples include:

- Closure commentary explicitly stating independent verification
- An action category that already labels the intervention
- A control-gap field containing the expected remediation
- Lifecycle comments written after later incidents occurred
- Current fields updated after issue closure
- Incident links added retrospectively

Before modelling, identify:

- Fields created or modified after the analytical anchor
- Fields visible to the current reviewer but not available at closure
- Fields that directly encode review outcomes
- Retrospective relationship changes
- Post-closure lifecycle commentary

Maintain two views:

1. Review-current view:
Everything legitimately available to the reviewer today.

2. Historical-as-at-closure view:
Only information known by the relevant date, where reconstructable.

The initial decision product may use the review-current view because it is triaging reviews today.

However, any later historical performance or predictive experiment must use an as-at view to avoid leakage.

