12. REFINE PROCESS AND APPLICATION RESOLUTION
======================================================================

A populated process or application field is not the same as a successfully resolved enterprise entity.

Profile separately:

- Field populated
- Identifier present
- Name only
- Exact match to master data
- Multiple possible matches
- Historic or retired entity
- Free-text value
- Unresolved value
- Hierarchy node available
- Entity relationship current at the relevant time, where history exists

Application and process names can change. Preserve stable identifiers where available.

If stable identifiers are absent, entity resolution becomes a separate risk and should not be hidden inside the retrieval pipeline.

======================================================================
13. REVISE THE PRIVACY CHECK
======================================================================

The privacy sample should not be limited to personal names, account numbers and obvious identifiers.

It should test whether text contains:

- Customer names
- Employee names
- Email addresses
- Telephone numbers
- Account and reference numbers
- Addresses
- Free-text health or vulnerability information
- Complaint details
- Other sensitive customer information
- Legal or investigation material

The product team should not independently define policy for these fields.

The profile should identify occurrence and recommend review by the appropriate privacy, data and model-risk functions.

The output should not reproduce sample sensitive content. Report only categories, rates and false-removal behaviour.

======================================================================
14. COUNTS BELOW 10: DISTINGUISH INTERNAL ANALYSIS FROM SHARED OUTPUT
======================================================================

Suppressing counts below 10 may be appropriate in shared artefacts, but blanket suppression can prevent internal quality analysis.

Use:

- Secure analytical output:
  Exact counts accessible only to authorised analysts.

- Shared profiling output:
  Suppressed or banded low counts where required.

The controlling privacy and disclosure standard should determine the threshold.

Do not treat 10 as universal unless it is confirmed by the relevant policy owner.

======================================================================
15. ADD ESTIMATED TOKEN AND COST PROFILING
======================================================================

Because the later stages use embeddings and reasoning models, profile:

- Character count
- Word count
- Estimated embedding tokens
- Estimated reasoning-model input tokens
- Distribution by object type and field
- Number of cases exceeding proposed payload limits
- Duplicate text that should not be reprocessed
- Hash-based unchanged records
- Expected candidate-incident text volume per issue

This is not only a cost exercise.

It determines:

- Whether element extraction requires chunking
- Whether action sets fit in a single reasoning call
- Whether lifecycle commentary should be reduced
- Whether retrieved incidents need staged summarisation
- Whether selected fields add useful evidence or only token volume

Keep structured metadata outside narrative payloads wherever possible.

======================================================================
16. ADD SNAPSHOT-SPECIFIC DEDUPLICATION RULES
======================================================================

The business has confirmed that REPORTING_DATE identifies the snapshot.

For current-state profiling:

1. Select the agreed maximum REPORTING_DATE independently for each table.
2. Confirm whether all tables share that date.
3. If not, report the date mismatch rather than silently mixing snapshots.
4. Within the selected snapshot, test one-row-per-business-ID.
5. If duplicates remain, identify the differentiating fields.
6. Do not automatically select one duplicate without a documented rule.
7. Preserve original row counts and deduplicated object counts.

For historical profiling:

- Retain each object by REPORTING_DATE.
- Do not confuse repeated snapshots with a newly created issue, action or incident.
- Use business IDs, not dimensional surrogate keys, for longitudinal tracking unless data owners state otherwise.

======================================================================
17. PROFILE THE NO-ACTION COHORT SEPARATELY
======================================================================

For closed issues with no linked action, profile:

- Count by domain, era and issue rating
- Source category
- Control-gap indicator
- Closure-summary usability
- Whether closure text explains why no action was required
- Whether an alternate remediation reference appears
- Whether Cross Ref and Issue association fields agree that no action exists

Do not include this cohort when calculating issue-action link accuracy or Tier 1 action-alignment feasibility.

Report it as a separate potential product branch.

======================================================================
18. DO NOT LIST THE TOP 1% OF UMBRELLA CASES BY COUNT ONLY AS THE MAIN TEST
======================================================================

Many linked actions may indicate an umbrella issue, but it may also indicate a legitimately complex material issue.

Use multiple signals:

- Action count
- Description breadth
- Number of distinct mechanisms identified during later labelling
- Scope breadth
- Programme language
- Multiple products, processes or applications
- Long duration
- Large number of linked incidents

At profiling stage, call these “complex or potential umbrella candidates”.

Do not automatically exclude them until the sponsor and SMEs agree on an operational definition.

For the first labelling experiment, complex cases may be excluded if action-set size makes comparison impractical, but the exclusion must be transparent.

